# TODO - tools/tidy lowerability contract

## Objective

Partition checks into language, ABI, runtime, determinism, and resource families
and enforce the promise that every accepted translation unit is supported by the
compiler for its declared target profile.

## Acceptance

- Freeze an explicit finite set of admitted C syntax/ABI/runtime families
  for the release, and prove each supported frontend-to-IR family lowers with
  representative positive/negative fixtures. All accepted fixtures compile;
  any rejected linter-clean program *within that declared subset* is a defect,
  not permission to silently shrink the subset or claim arbitrary C support.
  Later C features require a separately versioned admission contract.
- Accepted and rejected C fixtures exercise the boundary, and diagnostics
  identify the unsupported construct/profile requirement at source level.
- Rust tests are conformance/regression evidence for creating the profile; they
  do not replace manual `tools/tidy` validation of user-selected C.
- A rejection corresponds to a documented lowerability/determinism/profile
  requirement, not to arbitrary formatting or host-C style policy.
- No placeholder, hidden host substitution or undocumented workaround is
  permitted on this TODO's frozen admitted acceptance path. Unsupported or
  untested extensions fail explicitly and do not silently expand that path.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.
- **Finite acceptance witness:** One frozen supported-C feature/ABI table and at
  least one accepted and rejected source per entry; require exact
  toolchain/profile identity and finite source size, nesting and compiler-run
  budgets.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `tools/tidy/`, `scripts/validate/`,
  `src/compiler/`, `tests/compiler/`, `libc/`, `runtime/`,
  `docs/technical/specification/`, `tests/tidy/`.
- Required evidence: accepted/rejected source fixtures, source-located
  diagnostics, and compiler/linter contract regression tests.
- Transferred compiler-coverage obligation: typed IR deliberately fails closed
  on normalized C semantic shapes not yet lowered. Before this contract can be
  completed, all linter-clean accepted C fixtures must lower through the
  frontend and typed IR without widening host-dependent semantics.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `tools-tidy-clang-tidy-plugin`,
  `clang-c-frontend-integration`, `typed-compiler-ir`,
  `malbolge-layout-and-encoding-backend`, `supported-libc-contract`.
